<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Tinnitus: Points for Ringing in the Ears" description="Acupressure points traditionally used to ease the sensation of ringing or fullness in the ears, including GB20, GV20, LI4, LV3, and PC6. Safety guidance included." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Tinnitus", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-tinnitus/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Tinnitus: Points for Ringing in the Ears</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Tinnitus — a ringing, buzzing, or humming in the ears — is a symptom, not a disease, and it can be frustrating. A few points around the skull base, head, and hands are traditionally used to ease the sensation of fullness and support relaxation. This is comfort care, not a cure, and the first step for persistent tinnitus is a proper ear and hearing evaluation.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-forest-50 dark:bg-forest-900/20 border-l-4 border-forest-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Note:</strong> Evidence for acupressure in tinnitus is limited and inconsistent. Some small studies report modest relief of the perceived loudness or distress, but results are mixed. Think of it as a relaxation-based coping tool rather than a proven treatment.</p></div><section id="gb20" class="mb-8"><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Which Acupressure Points Help With Tinnitus?</h2><p class="mb-3">The points below are the ones most commonly used for this goal. Each links to a full guide with location, technique, and safety notes.</p></section><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. GB20 (Fengchi) - Wind Pool</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB20 at the base of the skull is traditionally used to clear the head and ears and release tension that often accompanies tinnitus.</p><h3 class="mb-3 font-semibold text-lg">How to Use GB20</h3><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the hollows at the base of the skull, between the two vertical neck muscles. <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press upward into the skull-base depressions with both thumbs for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section id="gv20" class="mb-8"><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Which Acupressure Points Help With Tinnitus?</h2><p class="mb-3">The points below are the ones most commonly used for this goal. Each links to a full guide with location, technique, and safety notes.</p></section><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. GV20 (Baihui) - Hundred Meetings</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GV20 at the crown is traditionally used to clear the head and support a calmer nervous system, which can lower the distress tinnitus causes.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the head, about two finger-widths behind the hairline at the top of the skull. <a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/">GV20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Gentle steady pressure with the fingertips for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Which Acupressure Points Help With Tinnitus?</h2><p class="mb-3">The points below are the ones most commonly used for this goal. Each links to a full guide with location, technique, and safety notes.</p></section><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 on the hand is a broad regulatory point traditionally used for head and face sensations, including ear discomfort.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the webbing between the thumb and index finger. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Squeeze the webbing firmly for 1-2 minutes per hand.</p></section><section id="lv3" class="mb-8"><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Which Acupressure Points Help With Tinnitus?</h2><p class="mb-3">The points below are the ones most commonly used for this goal. Each links to a full guide with location, technique, and safety notes.</p></section><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">4. LV3 (Taichong) - Great Surge</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LV3 on the foot is traditionally used to move stagnant energy and release tension that many people carry in the head and jaw.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the top of the foot, in the hollow between the first and second toes. <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly into the hollow for 1-2 minutes on each foot.</p></section><section id="pc6" class="mb-8"><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Which Acupressure Points Help With Tinnitus?</h2><p class="mb-3">The points below are the ones most commonly used for this goal. Each links to a full guide with location, technique, and safety notes.</p></section><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">5. PC6 (Neiguan) - Inner Gate</a></h2><p class="mb-3">PC6 on the inner forearm is traditionally used to calm the nervous system, which can reduce the stress and anxiety tinnitus often amplifies.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> About three finger-widths above the inner wrist crease, between the two forearm tendons. <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/">PC6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 1-2 minutes on each arm, breathing slowly.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Will it stop the ringing?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For most people it will not make tinnitus vanish. It may lower the tension and distress around it. Lasting relief depends on treating the underlying cause, which is why an ear exam matters.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">See a clinician promptly for tinnitus with sudden hearing loss, dizziness, facial weakness, one-sided ringing, or pulsatile (rhythmic) sounds. These can signal conditions that need medical attention.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Persistent tinnitus should be evaluated by a healthcare provider or audiologist.</p></div>
  <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011", "song-2015"]} note={"Evidence for acupressure from published systematic reviews. See our research page for details."} />
</article>
